WEBVTT

00:00.920 --> 00:02.740
- [Instructor] We're told that
Ricky is going on a road trip

00:02.740 --> 00:04.690
that is 100 kilometers long.

00:04.690 --> 00:08.880
His average speed is
70 kilometers per hour.

00:08.880 --> 00:11.760
At that speed, he can
drive five kilometers

00:11.760 --> 00:14.900
for every liter of fuel that he uses.

00:14.900 --> 00:16.010
Fuel costs

00:18.117 --> 00:21.010
.60 dollars per liter.

00:21.010 --> 00:22.700
So the equivalent of 60 cents per liter,

00:22.700 --> 00:25.690
but they wrote it as
.60 dollars per liter.

00:25.690 --> 00:28.290
What is the cost of fuel for the trip?

00:28.290 --> 00:30.693
Pause the video and see if
you can figure that out.

00:32.240 --> 00:34.560
All right, so let's see what
information they gave us.

00:34.560 --> 00:38.247
They tell us that the trip
is 100 kilometers long.

00:39.903 --> 00:42.663
They tell us that the average speed

00:42.663 --> 00:44.830
is 70 kilometers per hour.

00:46.190 --> 00:48.357
So 70 kilometers per hour.

00:50.503 --> 00:53.170
They tell us that at that speed,

00:55.080 --> 00:56.550
he can drive five kilometers

00:56.550 --> 00:58.910
for every liter of fuel that he uses.

00:58.910 --> 01:03.910
So five kilometers per liter.

01:04.100 --> 01:05.990
And then they tell us that fuel costs

01:05.990 --> 01:09.890
0.60 dollars per liter.

01:09.890 --> 01:13.570
So then this last piece of
information right over here

01:13.570 --> 01:18.570
is that fuel costs 0.60 dollars per liter.

01:19.160 --> 01:21.640
Normally we would see that
written as 60 cents per liter,

01:21.640 --> 01:23.380
but let's just go with it this way.

01:23.380 --> 01:26.340
So what's going to be
useful for the total cost

01:26.340 --> 01:28.163
of the fuel for the trip?

01:29.100 --> 01:30.470
Well, we need to figure out

01:30.470 --> 01:32.030
how much fuel we're going to use,

01:32.030 --> 01:35.170
and then multiply that,
times the cost of the fuel.

01:35.170 --> 01:39.380
So how much fuel are we going to be using?

01:39.380 --> 01:41.800
Let's see, we're going 100 kilometers,

01:41.800 --> 01:43.250
that's the total distance.

01:43.250 --> 01:46.430
And then this tells us,
essentially how many liters

01:46.430 --> 01:49.380
we're going to have to use
over those 100 kilometers.

01:49.380 --> 01:51.930
Now you might say, how
exactly does that work?

01:51.930 --> 01:55.320
Well if I'm going five
kilometers per liter,

01:55.320 --> 01:58.850
if I were to take the
reciprocal of this information,

01:58.850 --> 02:03.170
I would get one fifth of a liter,

02:03.170 --> 02:04.450
of a liter,

02:04.450 --> 02:06.800
per kilometer.

02:06.800 --> 02:09.560
That's how much fuel I use per kilometer.

02:09.560 --> 02:11.290
One fifth of a liter.

02:11.290 --> 02:13.030
And so why is that useful?

02:13.030 --> 02:15.713
Well if I take 100 kilometers,

02:17.620 --> 02:18.790
and if I were to multiply,

02:18.790 --> 02:21.520
times 1/5th of a liter per kilometer,

02:21.520 --> 02:22.900
this is going to tell you

02:22.900 --> 02:24.700
that over the course of this trip,

02:24.700 --> 02:28.560
I am going to use 100 times 1/5th liters.

02:28.560 --> 02:29.790
Or this is going to tell us

02:29.790 --> 02:31.200
that over the course of the trip,

02:31.200 --> 02:33.810
we're are going to use 20 liters.

02:33.810 --> 02:35.720
And then if we were to multiply that,

02:35.720 --> 02:38.120
times the cost of fuel per liter,

02:38.120 --> 02:40.920
well then we know how much
the cost of our trip is.

02:40.920 --> 02:42.210
So let's do that.

02:42.210 --> 02:44.220
Then let's multiply this,

02:44.220 --> 02:49.220
times 0.60 dollars per liter,

02:51.880 --> 02:53.430
which is the same thing
as multiplying this,

02:53.430 --> 02:57.727
times 0.60 dollars per liter.

03:00.550 --> 03:01.710
The liters cancel out,

03:01.710 --> 03:03.050
so it's good that our units work out.

03:03.050 --> 03:04.970
We're left with just dollars here.

03:04.970 --> 03:08.640
So 20 times 0.60

03:08.640 --> 03:13.360
is going to get us to 12.

03:13.360 --> 03:16.540
So we are left with 12 dollars.

03:19.110 --> 03:20.930
And we're done, that's
the cost of our trip.

03:20.930 --> 03:21.970
And I know what you're thinking.

03:21.970 --> 03:26.160
Wait, we didn't use the
information right over here,

03:26.160 --> 03:28.440
that he's traveling an average speed

03:28.440 --> 03:29.810
of 70 kilometers per hour.

03:29.810 --> 03:32.060
It's true, we did not use
it in our calculation.

03:32.060 --> 03:34.840
Although it was kind of
useful because we had to know

03:34.840 --> 03:37.320
what his fuel efficiency
is, at that speed.

03:37.320 --> 03:38.270
So they're saying, they're traveling

03:38.270 --> 03:39.330
at 70 kilometers per hour,

03:39.330 --> 03:42.610
then at that speed, we
get this fuel efficiency.

03:42.610 --> 03:43.780
Now they could have just told us,

03:43.780 --> 03:44.840
they didn't even have to tell us this,

03:44.840 --> 03:45.900
they could have just told us,

03:45.900 --> 03:47.030
at whatever speed he's going,

03:47.030 --> 03:48.730
his fuel efficiency is this.

03:48.730 --> 03:50.150
And we still would have
been able to figure out

03:50.150 --> 03:53.023
the total cost of the fuel for the trip.
